ios (database-java) failed. The workflow explicitly exits 1 when needs.scenarios.result is not success, so fixing the PR-attributable Cassandra library scenarios and stale coverage addresses this dependent failure without changing the gate.","decision":"fix","evidence":["Complete job name: required-status-check","##[group]Run exit 1","##[error]Process completed with exit code 1."],"key":"check:114960477855"},{"analysis":"All three Cassandra agent packages and existing JDBC packages passed; only the newly added Cassandra 4.19 library package failed all four scenarios for missing server.address and server.port. In published instrumentation 2.32.0-alpha, wrap(CqlSession) intentionally omits these stable attributes; wrap(CqlSession, Collection<InetSocketAddress>) derives them from the original contact points. The shared workload now supplies exactly the contact-point list used to build the session to the library callback, while agent callbacks still return the unwrapped session. The shared contract remains unchanged. Real Cassandra 5.0.9 runs passed all 16 Cassandra scenarios, with explicit assertions for the original address and port on spans and duration metrics. Regenerated library data.json adds the service.instance entity observed in the failed CI run, and a repeated library run produces identical coverage. The aggregate status failure is consequential, not a separate root cause.","decision":"fix","evidence":["==== instrumented: cassandra-driver-4.19, instrumentation: io.opentelemetry.instrumentation:opentelemetry-cassandra-4.4, package: opentelemetry-library","query: db.system.name='cassandra': server.address expected '127.0.0.1' on every span, saw [None]","query: db.system.name='cassandra': server.port expected on every span, set on 0/1","the committed data.json files are out of date","+    \"service.instance\": {"],"key":"check:114954314812"}],"input_identity":{"pr_diff_sha256":"6629b6e1a69cf7ecc0d782fe06717f9f8857763524747e5938661e2820049b53"},"outcome":"fixes"